  "Question": "If \\(1-y\\) is used as an approximation to the value of \\(\\dfrac1{1+y},\\) \\(|y|\\lt1,\\) the ratio of the error made to the correct value is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(y\\)",
    "B": "\\(y^2\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac1{1+y}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\dfrac y{1+y}\\)",
    "E": "\\(\\dfrac{y^2}{1+y}\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "B",
  "Solution": "First compare the exact and approximate values so that we know the sign of their difference. Let\n\\[\nT=\\frac1{1+y},\\qquad A=1-y.\n\\]\nSince \\(|y|<1\\), we have \\(1+y>0\\), so \\(T\\) is positive and nonzero. Their difference is\n\\[\nT-A\n=\\frac{1-(1-y)(1+y)}{1+y}\n=\\frac{y^2}{1+y}\\ge0.\n\\]\nThus the absolute error is \\(y^2/(1+y)\\). Dividing this by the correct value gives\n\\[\n\\frac{|T-A|}{T}\n=\\frac{y^2}{1+y}\\div\\frac1{1+y}\n=y^2.\n\\]\nThe formula also includes \\(y=0\\), when the approximation is exact. The required ratio is \\(y^2\\), choice B.",
